(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Asynchronous Programming in JavaScript, detailing why non-blocking asynchronous execution is vital for single-threaded web browsers.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   JavaScript in the browser runs on a single execution thread sharing the Call Stack with the UI) Tj
T*
(   rendering engine:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The Concurrency Problem:) Tj
T*
(   If a network request \(such as fetching a 10MB dataset\) were executed synchronously and) Tj
T*
(   blocking, the entire browser thread would halt for several seconds. During this freeze, the) Tj
T*
(   user cannot click buttons, scroll the page, or enter text, resulting in the dreaded 'Page) Tj
T*
(   Unresponsive' dialog.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   The Asynchronous Solution:) Tj
T*
(   Non-blocking asynchronous operations delegate time-consuming I/O tasks \(timers, network) Tj
T*
(   requests, disk operations\) to background browser C++ Web APIs. The single JavaScript thread) Tj
T*
(   continues executing surrounding synchronous code immediately without pausing. When the) Tj
T*
(   background I/O completes, the callback or Promise resolution is placed into the Event Loop) Tj
T*
(   queues \(Microtasks/Macrotasks\) to be executed as soon as the main Call Stack is clear.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the evolution of asynchronous JavaScript paradigms: Callbacks, Promises, and Async/Await.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Asynchronous JavaScript has undergone three generational architectural transformations:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Generation 1: Callbacks \(1995?2015\):) Tj
T*
(   - Asynchronous functions accepted a completion function pointer: `getData\(url, callback\)`.) Tj
T*
(   - Pitfalls: Resulted in 'Callback Hell' \(Pyramid of Doom\), fragmented error handling \(every) Tj
T*
(   callback required its own error parameter\), and inversion of control.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Generation 2: Promises \(ES6 / 2015\):) Tj
T*
(   - Encapsulated asynchronous operations into standardized objects with three states \(Pending,) Tj
T*
(   Fulfilled, Rejected\).) Tj
T*
(   - Advantages: Flattened nested pyramids into linear `.then\(\)` chains, centralized error) Tj
T*
(   handling via `.catch\(\)`, and provided concurrency combinators \(`Promise.all`\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Generation 3: Async / Await \(ES2017 / ES8\):) Tj
T*
(   - Built on top of Promises as syntactic sugar.) Tj
T*
(   - Advantages: Allows asynchronous code to be authored and read like clean, sequential) Tj
T*
(   synchronous code with standard `try...catch` blocks and linear variable assignments,) Tj
T*
(   drastically improving code maintainability.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
