12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze Security Vulnerabilities in AJAX applications: Cross-Origin Data Leakage, JSON Hijacking, and CSRF attacks.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   AJAX applications introduce unique attack surfaces requiring defense-in-depth:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. JSON Hijacking \(Historical Vulnerability\):) Tj
T*
(   Returning top-level JSON arrays \(`[{'secret': 'data'}]`\) allowed malicious sites to override) Tj
T*
(   the `Array` constructor and read data cross-origin via `<script src="/api/data">`. Mitigated by) Tj
T*
(   wrapping payloads in top-level JSON objects \(`{ 'data': [...] }`\) and prefixing with `\)]}',\\n`.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Cross-Site Request Forgery \(CSRF\):) Tj
T*
(   Because browsers automatically attach session cookies to cross-origin requests, malicious sites) Tj
T*
(   could issue background POST requests. Defended using `SameSite=Strict` cookies, custom headers) Tj
T*
(   \(`X-Requested-With`\), and anti-CSRF synchronizer tokens.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Cross-Origin Data Leakage via Misconfigured CORS:) Tj
T*
(   Reflecting incoming `Origin` headers into `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` without strict domain) Tj
T*
(   whitelisting allows unauthorized third-party sites to read sensitive user API responses.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 29:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
ET

